nlu@Xenon.Stanford.EDU (Nelson Lu) writes:

> In article <1993Jan4.140735.11269@wisipc.weizmann.ac.il> fedimit@wisipc.weizm
> >   Do you know that the expression NH-- > NH is always true in C ?
> >The reason is that NH-- returns NH before decrementing, and then
> >NH returns NH after decrementing.
> >   Similarly, NH++ < NH--
> 
> To be picky, I think if NH is so low that NH-- "wraps around" to a positive
> number, NH-- < NH.

It's even worse, C does not specify an evaluation order for
expressions. So it is perfectly legal if a compiler decides first
to evaluate NH and then NH--, thus making NH-- == NH.

In fact, according to the ANSI standard the decrementing of NH
may be postponed until the next sequence point (i.e. ; or , or ||
or && etc.) Thus implementing this as NH < NH; NH-- is legal.

The bottom line is that order of evaluation dependent side
effects will generally evaluate to bullshit. Check comp.lang.c
for details. It's in the FAQ.


